  sharing they're very clear they want to  see um you know some progress made and  that we at least get this bill  introduced and started but i know we  obviously be working very hard  to get them back in place they want to  see the issues around the protocol  settled and that's what this  government's determined to do  i i do want to ask a little bit about  the the cbi an organization which is  which has made a number of comments  about the northern ireland protocol over  well over the course of its lifetime  tony danker has been accused of  interfering in the government's plans to  deal with the protocol a couple of your  colleagues briefing the papers along  those lines do you share that view do  you think the cbi should you know sit  down and shut up  well look i work um very closely with  lots of different businesses and the  truth is that most of the major  retailers like marx and spencers and  tesco and others who are sending quite  complex composite loads of loras to  northern ireland they know that the  protocol if implemented in its in its um  uh full full way wouldn't it wouldn't  allow their business models to really  continue so uh businesses generally do  want us to sort this problem out  obviously cbi is a lobby group it can it  can set out its position um but we've  obviously  got access to the sort of right legal  advice as to what we're doing and we  obviously have a much more knowledge and  we we draw  um we draw a comment from a much wider  pool of businesses than the cbi  represents  at the rwanda repatriation flights uh  again heading towards the court of  appeal today it seems likely there will  be some form of legal action you know  over the coming weeks and months in  terms of all of this i mean one question  that keeps getting it keeps coming up to  me is why haven't you legislated to  allow the government to do this why have  you not legislated you know  passed a bill that for example you said  ignore these parts of the human rights  act i